{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791441591,"nonce":"Wi5RrPE1mS4wBHatCsVFASyjyqU=","title":"HIR: forward unchanged loads across branches to remove duplicate list bounds checks","message":"nbody_modified advance checks an index against a float-list length before reading, then checks the same index against the same unchanged length before writing. The two length loads are in consecutive success blocks, with no intervening memory mutation or call. Optimized HIR retains both loads and compares. Cranelift eliminates the second length load but its generated x86 code still performs the identical test/jle twice on the same register. This survives in the advance_resume0 OSR body too.\n\nWe already have branch_fold for dominating predicates. Its prerequisite is missing: HIR load_cse is intra-block only, so the two loads never become the same SSA value and ordinary CSE cannot unify the comparisons. This is separate from loop bounds versioning (92dbcd6).\n\nAdd conservative cross-block load forwarding through suitable CFG edges with explicit memory barriers, followed by CSE and branch folding. Preserve traps, error order, volatile/atomic accesses and aliasing stores; never carry a load through an unknown call or assume distinct pointer SSA values cannot alias. Cover no-write successor blocks, calls/writes, joins with differing memory state, loops and OSR continuation bodies. Verify duplicate conditional tests disappear in native output, then benchmark LLVM off without building between samples.\n\nEvidence: nbody_modified/hir/fn-advance-body.hir lines 140-157 and 177-183; extracted/advance-0.vcode lines 438-446 and 456-463. Source crates/compiler/src/load_cse.rs and branch_fold.rs.\n\nCaptured on the NUC (i5-1250P, x86-64) using saved fdf137cb52c3dc468a8c52d5bc88b1a6431a3a1c binaries, LLVM disabled, normal interpreter-first tiering. No builds ran. This is code-quality evidence, not a measured speedup. ZYNTAX_DUMP_CRANELIFT is input CLIF, before Cranelift optimization; the paired VCode dump is generated native code. OSR tracing was enabled, so total function call counts include instrumentation and cold paths.\n\nArtifacts: NUC /tmp/zyntax-clif-audit-fdf137cb and local /tmp/zyntax-clif-audit-fdf137cb-local. Script /tmp/zyntax-clif-audit.py. Local extracted/*.clif and *.vcode split the named dumps from stderr.log; optimized HIR is in each case's hir directory.","files":null}]}